Title: Livingston Hall
Order: 20
Production Code: 1x20

EXT. CHARLES RIVER - EVENING (WHAT REALLY HAPPENED)

AMB: lily stems, dark water, a canoe bottom-up

The public, and Mr. Vance, believe [[Molly Vance]] and [[Dianthe Lusk]] drowned. But as the three struggled in the water, Molly, with all the confidence of requited love, threw her arms about her lover. [[Aubrey Livingston]], with a muttered oath, tried to shake her off, but her clinging arms would not release him.

Over her shoulder he sees Dianthe's head disappearing beneath the lily-stems, and hears a heart-stirring cry of agony.

With a savage kick he frees himself and swims for the spot where Dianthe's golden head last appeared. He is just in time. Holding her head above water, he swims with her to the bank. Behind him, Molly sinks, without a word, and is covered by the glistening waters.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON
(staggering up the bank)
Dear God —

He looks back. Molly's body rises to the surface and floats, as did poor Elaine, her face lovely, as though she were asleep, and smiling.

EXT. COTTAGE UP THE BANK - NIGHT

A woman, evidently expecting him, opens the door before he knocks.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON
Quick! Here she is. Not a word. I will return tonight.

He sprints back to the riverbank, where the rescuers will find him seemingly exhausted — and, when he recovers, rumor will send him to Canada with a hunting party.

INT. LIVINGSTON HALL - DIANTHE'S ROOM - NIGHT (MONTHS LATER)

AMB: wind against the windows, a low fire

For weeks Dianthe lived in another world, unconscious of her own identity, quiescent in Aubrey's hands. It was early fall before her full faculties returned. One day he brought her a letter.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON
(gently, lying)
Reuel is dead, Dianthe.

Sick at heart, weary of the buffets of Fate, she yields to his persuasions. She marries him.

Now, in the first part of November, she lies pale by the fire as he comes to her.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON (CONT'D)
You are ill. I'm told you see spirits. Do they wear the dress of African explorers?

DIANTHE LUSK
(flat)
At this very hour, in this room — a woman stood where you're standing. She wrote in the Bible and vanished, same as before.

He crosses to the book. Underlined: the same verse from Luke. Signed this time "Nina," not "Mira." He says nothing, kisses her hand, and leaves.

Alone, moonlight catches an envelope on the floor — African postmark. She opens it.

JIM TITUS'S LETTER (V.O.)
Master Aubrey — twice I have trapped Dr. Briggs, and he has escaped me. I shall not fail the third time. I have suppressed all letters, as ordered. Put the first payment toward the five thousand in my sister's account. — Jim Titus.

She spends the night reading every letter Reuel ever sent her, hidden in Aubrey's own desk.

DIANTHE LUSK
(to herself, hollow)
Reuel is living. And I am a bigamist. Made so by fraud, and worse than fraud.

Something cold and clear settles over her — a name, once heard, for a poison subtle enough to leave no trace.

DIANTHE LUSK (CONT'D)
For me there's no retreat.
